WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.872 --> 00:00:10.334 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the South America capitals, episode three of three. First, I clear
the chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.984 --> 00:00:21.050 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
And four close the series: the new capital of Brazil in the interior, the
Chilean capital under the Andes, and the two cities of the Río de la Plata.

3
00:00:21.716 --> 00:00:24.607 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Brasília into view.

4
00:00:28.356 --> 00:00:36.111 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Brasília stands on the central plateau of Brazil, roughly a thousand
kilometres inland from the coast.

5
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:49.390 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Built in 41 months and inaugurated in 1960, designed by Lúcio Costa and Oscar
Niemeyer, it is the only city built in the twentieth century to be a UNESCO
World Heritage site.

6
00:00:50.055 --> 00:00:58.194 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Brasília as the city drawn on the plan and finished in 41 months, the
only new capital of the last century on UNESCO's list.

7
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.505 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Santiago into view.

8
00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:15.538 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Santiago lies in a valley of the Chilean Andes, about 570 metres above the
sea, with the snow-capped mountains rising straight behind it.

9
00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:31.141 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Founded in 1541 by the conquistador Pedro de Valdivia, it stayed a small town
until the nineteenth century; its Gran Torre Santiago, finished in 2013, was
then the tallest building in Latin America.

10
00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:38.653 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Santiago as the valley city under the Andes, and the tower that was
the tallest in Latin America.

11
00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:42.144 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Buenos Aires into view.

12
00:01:45.559 --> 00:01:52.804 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Buenos Aires stands on the pampas of eastern Argentina, on the western bank of
the wide Río de la Plata.

13
00:01:53.465 --> 00:02:11.645 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The province around the capital shares its name but is administered
separately, for the city is a district of its own. The pampas are among the
most fertile grasslands on earth, and their beef and grain made Argentina
briefly one of the world's richest countries.

14
00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:20.750 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Buenos Aires as the city on the silver river, the gaucho's plain, and
Martín Fierro, the national poem.

15
00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:24.579 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Montevideo into view.

16
00:02:28.016 --> 00:02:35.098 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Montevideo stands on the eastern shore of the Río de la Plata, facing Buenos
Aires across the wide water.

17
00:02:35.762 --> 00:02:55.778 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Where the name comes from is disputed; one old reading is "I see a mountain",
for the hill across the bay. Its Rambla runs along the river for more than
twenty kilometres, and its Palacio Salvo of 1928 was once the tallest building
in South America.

18
00:02:56.437 --> 00:03:08.465 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Montevideo as the city opposite Buenos Aires, with the long Rambla
and the 1928 tower that was the tallest in South America.

19
00:03:13.453 --> 00:03:23.647 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Brasília stands alone in the central plateau, Santiago lies under the snow of
the Andes, and Buenos Aires and Montevideo face each other across the silver
river.

20
00:03:24.301 --> 00:03:34.448 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the forty-one months of Brasília, the tower of Santiago under the
Andes, the pampas and Martín Fierro of Buenos Aires, and the Rambla of
Montevideo.
